# Lead-lens sign-off — fix/489-audit-log-survives-a-reset
- date: 2026-11-08
- lead: saltzer-schroeder
- findings cleared:
  - F1 (self, RED) — my tests wrote 24 lines into the operator's real record
    at ~/.bassclef/publish-gate-audit.log. The pre-existing run() helper sets
    PUBLISH_GATE_AUDIT only, so the new local write escaped to $HOME. Found by
    running `status` against the live environment, not by reading the harness.
    Every runner now redirects HOME as well as both paths; T31 pins the
    default path so the redirect is meaningful; the real record was restored
    from the mirror and verified byte-identical across a full suite run.
  - F2 (self, RED) — my first pairing check reported the log's own first line
    as a defect. The log opens with an `on` from 2026-11-05 because the clear
    before it was a raw API call made while `off` was broken (#481), so no
    `off` line could exist. A check that cries wolf on its opening line every
    run stops being read. A leading `on` now reads as informational; T30 pins
    that a later lone restore is still caught.
  - F3 (self, amber) — T23's fixture was a single `on` line, which the
    calibration correctly reclassifies as informational. The fixture was too
    thin to tell the two cases apart. Widened to put the lone restore mid-log.
- shown RED before GREEN:
  - 16 assertions across T19-T28 failed against the old script, with
    `unknown verb 'audit'` and an empty local log
  - T29/T30 failed before the calibration; T31 failed before the HOME redirect
  - ran the check against the REAL log with the reconstructed line removed:
    it names 2026-11-08T02:25:49Z as a restore with no clear before it, which
    is exactly the hole #489 was filed for
- verdict: cleared for merge.
